// Marks a type as "numeric" for defaulting/constraint purposes — see
// cleave/src/infer.rs's module docs on the numeric-literal gap this exists
// to eventually close (a literal's type variable should check membership
// here instead of defaulting blindly, regardless of what it got unified
// with along the way).
//
// No axioms/methods required — a pure existence-marker algebra. Nothing
// implements `Num` directly here — every concrete numeric type reaches it
// through `Int`/`Float`'s own bound below (`Infer::has_matching_impl`'s
// bound-inheritance walk: an `impl Int<i32>` counts as `Num<i32>` too,
// exactly because `algebra Int<T> : Num` says so).
algebra Num<T> {}

// `Int`/`Float` split `Num` along the one distinction a bare numeric
// literal's own text already carries (`1` vs `1.0`) — the language has no
// implicit conversions anywhere, integers and floats included, so a literal
// written with a `.` genuinely cannot become an integer type and vice versa;
// this is what makes that a real, checkable constraint rather than a
// defaulting-only hint. Each bounds on `Num` — an `impl Int<i32>` (below)
// is *all* that's declared for `i32`; no separate `impl Num<i32>` exists or
// is needed, `has_matching_impl` derives it from the bound.
algebra Int<T> : Num {}
algebra Float<T> : Num {}

impl Int<i8> {}
impl Int<i16> {}
impl Int<i32> {}
impl Int<i64> {}

impl Float<f32> {}
impl Float<f64> {}
